  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Resilience Fund supported evaluations of the COVID-19 cash transfer program for poor and vulnerable households which identified the need to extend support to near-poor households.  This resulted in the Government extending IDPoor to near-poor households benefitting an additional 1,340,942 people including 710,467 women. The investment also provided technical advice and support to the RGC to design a multi-tiered pension system, and to design and pilot a national multi-dimensional vulnerability index to track development progress measures of income. The draft framework Australia supported has been completed and endorsed by the RGC for implementation.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The program supported Cambodia&#x2019;s COVID-19 stimulus package to ensure it accurately targeted vulnerable groups. This resulted in 381,231 new households receiving an IDPoor card and the cash transfer for poor and vulnerable households. Over US$932 million was transferred to 706,648 families including 64,000 persons with disabilities (31,366 women with disabilities) and around 2,000 people living with HIV (1024 women). The Government launched a new cash transfer program for at-risk households affected by rising inflation and floods, enrolling an additional 357,869 households (1,340,942 people; 710,467 women) and ensuring 90,893 people (47,749 women)) received IDPoor cards.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
